-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Exposure Variation:** Scenes with severe exposure variation in training views can cause the method to produce clear brightness boundaries and many "floaters" (artifacts), degrading PSNR and FPS [S1].
-*   **Memory Usage:** The method uses more voxel primitives than Gaussian splatting, resulting in larger model size and higher GPU memory requirements [S1]. Uniform high-resolution grids (e.g., $1024^3$) can cause Out Of Memory (OOM) errors on standard hardware (e.g., 24GB GPU) [S1].
-*   **Depth Quantization:** Pathological voids or highly non-uniform depth distributions may impact bin estimation for depth-quantile pruning [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Adaptive Voxel Size
-A technique where voxels are subdivided adaptively based on detail requirements, as opposed to uniform subdivision until a fixed grid resolution is reached [S1]. On the Mip-NeRF360 dataset, adaptive voxel size achieves LPIPS 0.200, PSNR 28.01, and FPS 171, while uniform $512^3$ achieves LPIPS 0.326, PSNR 25.37, and FPS 190 [S1]. Uniform $256^3$ achieves LPIPS 0.444, PSNR 23.98, and FPS 457 [S1]. Uniform $1024^3$ causes Out Of Memory (OOM) on a machine with 24GB GPU memory [S1]. The adaptive variant outperforms Plenoxels ($640^3$ grid), which achieves LPIPS 0.452, PSNR 23.29, and FPS <10 [S1].
+Adaptive voxel size subdivides voxels based on detail requirements rather than reaching a fixed uniform grid resolution. It achieves higher quality (LPIPS 0.200, PSNR 28.01) than uniform 512$^3$ grids (LPIPS 0.326, PSNR 25.37) while using less memory, avoiding OOM errors seen at 1024$^3$ resolution. It outperforms Plenoxels (640$^3$) in both quality and speed [S1].
+
+### SVRecon
+SVRecon generalizes SVRaster to surface reconstruction by parametrizing each voxel with SDF values at its corners. It uses trilinear interpolation for SDFs, NeuS CDF for alpha blending, and bitmask-based neighbor search for Laplacian smoothness. It achieves better Chamfer distance on DTU (5 mm vs 6 mm) and higher F1 score on Tanks-and-Temples than SVRaster density-based methods [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Speed:** On Mip-NeRF360, the base method achieves 121 FPS [S1]. A fast-render variant sets $h_{ss}=1.5, h_{prune}=0.15$ for >2x FPS with moderate quality reduction [S1]. On Deep Blending, it achieves 366 FPS [S1]. It is comparable to 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) on average, though scene-dependent [S1].
-*   **Training Time:** Training takes approximately 15 minutes on Mip-NeRF360 [S1]. A fast-train variant scales iteration-related hyperparameters by 0.3 for >3x training speedup [S1].
-*   **Quality:** Achieves significantly better LPIPS than 3DGS and surpasses Zip-NeRF on Mip-NeRF360 [S1]. PSNR tends to favor 3DGS as it prefers smoother rendering in uncertain regions [S1].
-*   **Memory:** Requires more GPU memory than 3DGS due to the larger number of voxel primitives [S1]. LiteVoxel variant reduces peak VRAM by up to 60% while maintaining comparable perceptual metrics (PSNR ≈ 32.13 dB, SSIM ≈ 0.937) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Scalability:** Rendering speed scales well to higher resolutions due to fewer contributing primitives per pixel [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Performance degrades on scenes with severe exposure variation [S1]. Resolution-memory trade-offs become pronounced at deep Levels of Detail (LoDs) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering speed is comparable to 3D Gaussian Splatting, with FPS varying by scene. Sorting 48-bit Morton codes is used for primitive ordering [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses significantly less memory than dense grids (e.g., 1024$^3$ causes OOM on 24GB GPU) but more than Gaussian splatting due to voxel primitives [S1]. LiteVoxel variant reduces peak VRAM by up to 60% compared to standard SVRaster [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** On Mip-NeRF-360, achieves 121 FPS with 15 minutes training time, LPIPS 0.185, PSNR 27.33 [S1]. Fast-render variant achieves >2x FPS with moderate quality reduction [S1]. Fast-train variant achieves >3x speedup [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High resolution scalability, real-time rendering, explicit geometry extraction (SVRecon).
+*   **Limitations:** Struggles with severe exposure variations in training views, leading to brightness boundaries and floaters [S1]. Disjoint voxel design requires explicit continuity losses [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Sparse Voxel Grid Optimization is based on the Sparse Voxel Rasterization (SVRaster) method [S1]. It was introduced in the paper "Sparse Voxels Rasterization: Real-time High-fidelity Radiance Field Rendering" [S1]. The method builds on hierarchical grid optimization techniques and aims to provide real-time rendering capabilities with high fidelity [S1]. It is compatible with grid-based algorithms such as Volume Fusion, Voxel Pooling, and Marching Cubes [S2, unofficial].
+Sparse Voxel Grid Optimization originated from the need to combine the high fidelity of neural radiance fields with the efficiency of sparse voxel structures. It was introduced in "Sparse Voxels Rasterization: Real-time High-fidelity Radiance Field Rendering" (CVPR 2025) [S1]. The method builds on hierarchical grid optimization principles [1414-hierarchical-grid-optimization.md] and extends them with dynamic pruning and subdivision. SVRecon, a variant for surface reconstruction, was later proposed by Oh et al. (2025) [S2, unofficial].
 
